name wordnet-data
# The database files, one for each syntactic category (wndb.5WN, "Data File Format"): "Each data file begins with
# several lines containing a copyright notice, version number and license agreement. These lines all begin with two
# spaces and the line number". Then a synset a line:
#   synset_offset  lex_filenum  ss_type  w_cnt  word  lex_id  [word  lex_id...]  p_cnt  [ptr...]  [frames...]  |   gloss
# "w_cnt: Two digit hexadecimal integer indicating the number of words in the synset"; "p_cnt: Three digit decimal
# integer indicating the number of pointers from this synset to other synsets"; a pointer is "pointer_symbol
# synset_offset pos source/target"; "frames: In data.verb only, a list of numbers corresponding to the generic verb
# sentence frames for words in the synset. frames are of the form: f_cnt + f_num w_num [+ f_num w_num...]"; "gloss:
# Each synset contains a gloss. A gloss is represented as a vertical bar (|), followed by a text string that continues
# until the end of the line." The field names are wndb.5's, this recipe's handles: none is in any claim.
match data.noun data.verb data.adj data.adv

tier row by \n
comment space
remark | gloss
names synset_offset lex_filenum ss_type w_cnt p_cnt f_cnt
counted w_cnt radix 16 word word lex_id
counted p_cnt pointer pointer_symbol target pos source_target
counted f_cnt frame plus f_num w_num
tier field by space

# A synset is the concept it is: "synset_offset: Current byte offset in the file represented as an 8 digit decimal
# integer", unique only with its type, is WordNet's pointer to it, which CILI keys as the offset and the letter of its
# type (ili.ttl: dc:source pwn30:00001740-n; a satellite's letter is s). Resolved through the highway, recorded nowhere.
type synset_offset ili as \0-{ss_type}
thing row synset_offset
# "word: ASCII form of a word as entered in the synset by the lexicographer, with spaces replaced by underscore
# characters (_)": content, each a word of the synset in the order the synset lists them.
thing word word
# one piece, in which the file writes _ for a space
part word by \t space _
content word
# wndb.5: w_cnt, "the number of words in the synset": each a word in the synset
relation "words in the synset" word
holds row word
# "lex_id: One digit hexadecimal integer that, when appended onto lemma, uniquely identifies a sense within a
# lexicographer file": a part of WordNet's pointer to the sense (its sense key), recorded nowhere.
omit lex_id
# "ss_type: One character code indicating the synset type": n NOUN, v VERB, a ADJECTIVE, s ADJECTIVE SATELLITE, r ADVERB
content ss_type
value - ss_type
attest row ss_type
# "lex_filenum: Two digit decimal integer corresponding to the lexicographer file name containing the synset": the
# lexicographer file lexnames lists by that number (lexnames.recipe keys it), a value of the highway's lexfile list.
type lex_filenum lexfile
value lexfile lex_filenum
attest row lex_filenum
# the gloss: "may contain a definition, one or more example sentences, or both" (wndb.5)
content gloss
relation "a definition, one or more example sentences, or both" gloss
attest row gloss
# A pointer: wndb.5, "pointer_symbol: the symbol for the pointer type" (the table in wninput.5WN: @ Hypernym,
# ~ Hyponym, ! Antonym, ...), the relation as WordNet writes it, of WordNet's own inventory; "synset_offset ... pos" is
# the synset it points to, as the synset's own offset and type are; "source/target ... 0000 means that pointer_symbol
# represents a semantic relation between the current (source) synset and the target synset indicated by
# synset_offset". A lexical pointer (source/target not 0000: "the word number in the current (source) synset ... and
# the word number in the target synset") is between two words, an unresolved obligation (Laplace-Engine#70).
content pointer_symbol
value - pointer_symbol
type target ili as \0-{pos} as \0-s
omit pos
when source_target is 0000
relate pointer pointer_symbol to target
always
# A verb synset's frames: wndb.5, "f_num: Two digit decimal integer frame number" of frames.vrb's generic sentence frames,
# and "w_num: ... the word in the synset that the frame applies to ... 00 ... all words in the synset": f_num is
# frames.vrb's key, which the frames recipe (Laplace-Engine#70, next) keys; until it does, f_num and w_num are left
# open, and said to be when data.verb is read. A lexical pointer (source/target not 0000, between two words) says
# nothing yet: the pointer's relation is said only under 'when source_target is 0000' below (Laplace-Engine#70).
# the counts are how many groups follow: the file's layout, read by nothing
omit w_cnt p_cnt f_cnt plus